<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  CRIT-3 FIX: Explicit Network Security Configuration.
  - Trusts only system CAs — no user-installed CAs (prevents corp-MITM attacks).
  - Prevents accidental plain-text connections even on Android 6-8 where the
    platform default could allow cleartext in some edge cases.

  SCOPE NOTE (do not misread this as app-wide cleartext protection): the
  cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" below is enforced only by Android's
  standard network stack — HttpURLConnection/HttpsURLConnection, WebView,
  and any library that calls through to it (OkHttp does, for example, via
  android.security.NetworkSecurityPolicy). In this app that covers RESTCONF,
  Redfish, the RD Web feed client, and Guacamole's WebSocket/REST legs.

  It does NOT cover the raw java.net.Socket/SSLSocket connections this app's
  core remote-access protocols use directly — Telnet, VNC, RDP (native
  FreeRDP bridge), IPMI, AMT SOL/IDER/KVM, SNMP, rlogin, Mosh, the serial
  console, and NFS/ONC-RPC all open sockets themselves and never consult
  NetworkSecurityPolicy, so this config has zero effect on whether *those*
  connections are cleartext. Several of them (plain Telnet, VNC, unencrypted
  RDP) are cleartext by design/user choice, wrapped in TLS/SSH only when the
  person explicitly enables it per profile (`useTls`, "tunnel via SSH", etc.)
  — that per-protocol opt-in, not this file, is what actually governs
  encryption for those transports. Do not rely on this file as evidence that
  those protocols are protected against cleartext.

  ENFORCEMENT FOR THE RAW-SOCKET PROTOCOLS (CRIT-3-B): since this file
  architecturally cannot cover them, the actual enforcement mechanism is
  tools/verify_socket_security.py — a Gradle task (`verifySocketSecurity`,
  wired into `check`) that scans app/src/main/java for every raw
  Socket/DatagramSocket/ServerSocket construction and requires each one to
  be on record in tools/socket_security_manifest.json with a reviewed
  justification (protocol-inherent cleartext, TOFU-pinned TLS wrap,
  loopback-only, own crypto layer, etc.). A new or changed call site that
  isn't accounted for there fails the build. See that script's module
  docstring for the full rationale.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- Trust only system CA store — user-installed CAs are excluded. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
